\begin{abstract}
Quantum spin ice has a well-established long-wavelength description in
terms of an emergent photon, and previous exact-diagonalization and
semiclassical work already establishes that the underlying compact
gauge theory is strongly interacting. What those results do not fix
is how the microscopic ring model distributes its longitudinal
spectral weight at finite momentum. We compute the zero-temperature
correlation function $\Szz(\bq,\omega)$ of the local Ising pseudospin,
which is exactly the emergent electric field, in the ground-state
winding sector of 48-, 64- and 72-site pyrochlore clusters, resolving every
eigenstate and matrix element rather than a broadened lineshape. The
response is photon-like at the smallest accessible momentum and
reconstructs at the zone boundary. At $L$ a collective level appears
at $1.02K$ on 48 sites, $0.95K$ on 64 and $1.07K$ on 72, carries at least half
the weight at that momentum, and lies near half the Gaussian-photon
energy. A basis-independent polarization analysis identifies it as
the lower of the two transverse branches rather than a new species,
and few-photon kinematics place it a factor of five below the
three-photon threshold. We call the mode roton-like in the Feynman
sense: its softening tracks the ratio of oscillator strength to
equal-time structure factor, $f_1(\bq)/S(\bq)$, both of which are
ground-state quantities, and the ratio correctly predicts in advance
which of two inequivalent $L$-type momentum classes on the 64-site
cluster reconstructs. Two microscopic diagnostics separate the level
from an ordinary dressed photon: a Rokhsar--Kivelson flippability
potential, used only as an infinitesimal probe at $V=0$, shows that it
gains flippable hexagons while losing ring-exchange coherence; and an
exact plaquette decomposition attributes $71\%$ of its variational
relaxation to the one hexagon family that the electric-field wave
leaves entirely unconstrained, a lattice analogue of backflow. The
same reconstruction appears as an order-one polarization splitting at
the zone boundary, and a further $16$--$38\%$ of the weight lies in
levels absent from the Gaussian one-photon theory. We state
explicitly which conclusions are limited by the accessible cluster
sizes.
\end{abstract}

